1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
They've cut enemy infantry to ribbons and blasted aircrafts out of the sky.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:10,000
The unstoppable cadence of these machines now dominate the battlefield.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:15,000
From Gatling's rotary gun to modern weapons that blaze away hundreds of rounds a minute,

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:20,000
nothing can withstand the firepower of the machine gun, now on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,800
This weapon that fires round after round after round

6
00:00:34,801 --> 00:00:37,480
has placed in the hands of the infantryman

7
00:00:37,481 --> 00:00:39,960
more power than he has ever held before.

8
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:44,260
That power has made its presence felt on many battlefields.

9
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:50,260
Continually refined and strengthened from the First World War to Desert Storm,

10
00:00:50,261 --> 00:00:53,840
the first significant improvement on Puckold's flint block

11
00:00:53,841 --> 00:00:56,900
was an invention by Dr. Richard Gatlin.

12
00:00:56,901 --> 00:01:06,580
With the Gatling, you have the first heavy machine gun that's effective on the battlefield.

13
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:14,480
The Gatling gun is hand-cranked, and you can get out about 300 rounds a minute.

14
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:20,860
A bullet drops into the top barrel and is locked in place as the crank rotates the barrels.

15
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:25,860
When the loaded barrel reaches the bottom position, a firing pin strikes the bullet.

16
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:31,000
After that barrel has fired, the next loaded barrel moves into position and fires.

17
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:41,600
Unfortunately, it's not adopted by the Union Army because General Ripley, the chief of ordnance during the period, isn't sold on it.

18
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:47,760
He has 198 different calibers of ammunition that he has to supply to the Union Army.

19
00:01:47,761 --> 00:01:53,200
He's trying to solve his ammunition problem, so he's not really interested in something like this.

20
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:56,340
Gatling guns were used on a limited scale.

21
00:01:56,900 --> 00:02:08,080
But it wasn't until 1885 when another American, Hiram Maxim, perfected a weapon that could fire 600 shots a minute with no more effort than a finger on a trigger.

22
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:14,120
Maxim used the energy of the gun's recoil to load, cock, fire, and eject shells.

23
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:19,840
As Maxim's gun is fired, the recoil impact forces the breech block backwards.

24
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:23,380
The breech lock then moves forward.

25
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:27,580
Maxim's invention was received with great enthusiasm in Europe.

26
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:33,440
By 1914, Germany had over 12,000 machine guns in service with her army.

27
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:38,100
When the machine gun came to be used during the Great War,

28
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:48,300
it was seen as a heavy support weapon laying down a blanket of fire to support and protect the infantry.

29
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:57,700
For this reason, the machine guns were built as heavy guns capable of firing for extended periods of time.

30
00:02:58,540 --> 00:03:01,800
As a result, most of the machine guns were water-cooled.

31
00:03:02,420 --> 00:03:07,400
The barrel was surrounded by a large cylindrical water jacket.

32
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:14,600
There was a hose connected to a condenser tank that circulated water back into the water jacket so the gun would not run dry.

33
00:03:14,601 --> 00:03:19,960
surrounding the barrel with water improved the gun's effectiveness

34
00:03:19,961 --> 00:03:27,600
water-cooled machine guns were capable of firing for long periods of time several hundred rounds

35
00:03:27,601 --> 00:03:35,860
could be fired before the gun had to be allowed to cool normally you have a crew of two you have

36
00:03:35,861 --> 00:03:41,160
the gunner and you have an assistant gunner who is responsible for the ammunition supply you would

37
00:03:41,161 --> 00:03:47,580
You need three men to carry a .50 caliber gun into battle.

38
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:51,060
Two men could do it, but they'd be terribly loaded.

39
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:56,520
Even seasoned gunners struggle to keep targets within their sights.

40
00:03:57,860 --> 00:04:04,480
A good machine gunner is an artist at firing a weapon.

41
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,080
It's pretty jarring. It shakes the living daylights out of you.

42
00:04:09,081 --> 00:04:12,980
And it just doesn't sit there where you are aiming.

43
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:17,400
You have to hold on to that trigger and the handle,

44
00:04:18,340 --> 00:04:22,880
and it just shakes your whole body while you're firing it.

45
00:04:22,881 --> 00:04:29,940
Once you start firing, the gunner will sit there normally holding on to the spade trails

46
00:04:29,941 --> 00:04:31,440
and working the triggers.

47
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:36,440
Every now and again, he will reach up and he'll bang the gun on one side,

48
00:04:36,441 --> 00:04:41,320
And what he's doing, he's traversing the gun with what is known as the T&E mechanism,

49
00:04:41,480 --> 00:04:43,480
or the traversing and elevation mechanism.

50
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:46,500
And that's what makes a machine gun so effective.

51
00:04:46,920 --> 00:04:53,100
The gun in and of itself fires fast, but the thing that keeps it on target is the T&E mechanism.

52
00:04:56,660 --> 00:05:01,680
Employed in pairs, the machine guns swept a stream of fire back and forth over the battlefield.

53
00:05:01,681 --> 00:05:11,000
So you can quite literally have a curtain of steel in front of your position.

54
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:15,240
And you combine that with barbed wire that holds up the other guy,

55
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:20,080
and you have an effective way to stop these infantry attacks.

56
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:30,380
Machine guns left the infantrymen no choice but to dig in and fight for survival from inside trenches.

57
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:38,180
We were very afraid.

58
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:40,460
A machine got fire.

59
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:46,900
And the sound was terrifying because we'd never heard anything like that.

60
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:53,700
When you hear that, and you're laying on your belly in the mud somewhere,

61
00:05:53,701 --> 00:06:04,660
You hear the leaves and the branches all around you have fallen and been clipped off by these staccato bursts that come from all directions.

62
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:10,200
It seems they sound mean and dangerous.

63
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:15,820
We just prayed that we wouldn't get hit.

64
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:22,560
You never think that it's going to hit you or it's going to hit the other guy.

65
00:06:22,561 --> 00:06:30,620
You feel stark naked, and you see dirt flying all around you.

66
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:38,060
You see men falling, and it's a sensation, and the guns keep barking.

67
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:41,280
There's no place you can hide.

68
00:06:42,640 --> 00:06:48,520
You just have to pray to the good Lord that it's not going to hit you.

69
00:06:48,521 --> 00:06:51,720
you'll always see somebody falter

70
00:06:51,721 --> 00:06:54,700
go down.

71
00:06:55,680 --> 00:06:57,620
You'll see them hung up in wire

72
00:06:57,621 --> 00:06:59,380
just shot to be riddled.

73
00:07:00,220 --> 00:07:02,140
They hung up, couldn't get out

74
00:07:02,141 --> 00:07:05,700
so the gutters would take target practice on them.

75
00:07:06,840 --> 00:07:09,800
It's a grisly thing

76
00:07:09,801 --> 00:07:13,420
that's hard to believe

77
00:07:13,421 --> 00:07:16,180
until it's around you.

78
00:07:16,181 --> 00:07:18,500
You could see it yourself.

79
00:07:21,120 --> 00:07:25,740
It was a measure of poor generalship that for much of World War I,

80
00:07:26,460 --> 00:07:31,560
the general stats could think of nothing better than sending ranks of infantry or even cavalry

81
00:07:31,561 --> 00:07:38,840
into masses of German heavy machine guns laying down fire

82
00:07:38,841 --> 00:07:44,500
that often wiped out entire companies of infantry as they advanced across no man's land.

83
00:07:44,501 --> 00:07:46,734
It was just plain murder.

84
00:07:46,735 --> 00:07:55,940
Pack up your gear and go over to the top for all daylight right in the face of these murderous weapons.

85
00:07:57,340 --> 00:08:03,480
But even in the face of horrific machine gun fire, Winston Roche went over the top.

86
00:08:03,481 --> 00:08:10,880
I didn't know that I was hit by a machine gun.

87
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:16,200
The only thing I'd do, I thought somebody had pushed me

88
00:08:16,201 --> 00:08:22,260
because I was knocked sideways and fell face down into the mud.

89
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:26,220
And the bullet went through my side,

90
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:30,980
passed between two of my ribs and out.

91
00:08:30,981 --> 00:08:44,981
I lay there gasping for breath from the shock and the fright.

92
00:08:47,340 --> 00:08:55,240
And I'm sure I wet my pants because I could feel it warm down both legs.

93
00:08:56,500 --> 00:08:59,220
And it was the blood.

94
00:09:00,980 --> 00:09:05,520
Heavy machine guns paralyzed the battlefield.

95
00:09:06,260 --> 00:09:12,900
If the infantry was ever to advance and gain ground, they needed a more mobile weapon, a light machine gun.

96
00:09:15,280 --> 00:09:22,600
Light machine guns were developed for use by small infantry units, typically squads or sections.

97
00:09:23,620 --> 00:09:29,220
What the light machine gun did was increase the firepower of the infantry unit,

98
00:09:29,221 --> 00:09:32,920
and because it was either handheld or mounted on a bipod,

99
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,080
it didn't have the accuracy of the heavy machine gun.

100
00:09:35,920 --> 00:09:37,960
Instead of Maxim's recoil system,

101
00:09:38,340 --> 00:09:42,420
light machine guns used a small portion of the gases produced by the fired round

102
00:09:42,421 --> 00:09:43,960
to force back a piston.

103
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:46,900
The piston, in turn, pushed back the breech block

104
00:09:46,901 --> 00:09:48,740
and readied the gun to fire again.

105
00:09:49,560 --> 00:09:53,060
The most successful gas-operated gun was the Lewis gun.

106
00:09:54,100 --> 00:09:56,060
The British Lewis light machine gun

107
00:09:56,061 --> 00:09:59,180
was regarded as one of the best weapons of the First World War.

108
00:10:00,180 --> 00:10:07,100
The Americans in World War I originally had been issued British Lewis guns, which they liked very much.

109
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:10,620
And then these were taken away and replaced with French weapons.

110
00:10:11,580 --> 00:10:17,320
The French give us a light machine gun called the Chaucho.

111
00:10:18,020 --> 00:10:25,580
It is beyond a doubt the worst piece of garbage ever inflicted on the American soldier.

112
00:10:25,581 --> 00:10:34,020
the state of manufacture in these weapons was so bad that you couldn't take one and put the

113
00:10:34,021 --> 00:10:42,500
parts in another and make the other one work it was just awful we'll be using guns that have been

114
00:10:42,501 --> 00:10:49,800
in action patrol for three years the show show was a good gun it was very simple you could

115
00:10:49,801 --> 00:10:55,789
disassemble it very quickly and seem more or less as the lowest.

116
00:10:55,790 --> 00:10:57,660
But after these guns

117
00:10:57,661 --> 00:11:06,360
have been in existence and used, fired thousands of rounds, they were worn out.

118
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:14,020
They cut holes in the side of the magazine so the soldiers could see how much ammunition

119
00:11:14,021 --> 00:11:20,760
they had left, but in the trenches, you get sand and dirt and filth inside these magazines,

120
00:11:21,200 --> 00:11:24,780
and it just didn't work.

121
00:11:26,900 --> 00:11:34,120
The MP5N Heckler and Koch 9mm submachine guns are considered the main weapon in closed-quarters

122
00:11:34,121 --> 00:11:35,180
battle by the Marines.

123
00:11:35,700 --> 00:11:40,280
They cost $894 each and fire 800 rounds per minute.

124
00:11:40,860 --> 00:11:42,460
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

125
00:11:44,020 --> 00:11:47,840
Machine guns set up in defensive positions

126
00:11:47,841 --> 00:11:51,160
turned the Great War into a quagmire fought from trenches.

127
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:55,220
But it was also the machine guns, this time in armored vehicles,

128
00:11:55,420 --> 00:11:56,680
that broke the stalemate.

129
00:12:00,280 --> 00:12:03,260
The tank originated as really nothing more

130
00:12:03,261 --> 00:12:07,180
than a way to safely move a machine gun across no man's land.

131
00:12:07,181 --> 00:12:12,440
you could drive them right in

132
00:12:12,441 --> 00:12:14,760
to and against

133
00:12:14,761 --> 00:12:16,660
a pillbox

134
00:12:16,661 --> 00:12:18,760
you hear the bullets

135
00:12:18,761 --> 00:12:21,060
digging off the sides

136
00:12:21,061 --> 00:12:22,120
as I say

137
00:12:22,121 --> 00:12:25,500
just beating a tattoo on the underneath side

138
00:12:25,501 --> 00:12:28,640
very very scary

139
00:12:28,641 --> 00:12:30,980
and the noise is deafening

140
00:12:30,981 --> 00:12:34,000
they were fun to drive

141
00:12:34,001 --> 00:12:37,300
and I poured it fire right in.

142
00:12:37,301 --> 00:12:45,420
I tried to hit the slits and it did silence the gun.

143
00:12:47,520 --> 00:12:51,700
Hitting anything from the rickety tanks required much gunnery practice.

144
00:12:55,720 --> 00:13:00,000
It's very difficult to aim them properly.

145
00:13:00,001 --> 00:13:05,960
See, the guns were fixed, and you could lose the flaps,

146
00:13:06,480 --> 00:13:11,720
but you'd have to be standing still because you couldn't drive it and aim it.

147
00:13:11,840 --> 00:13:14,520
You had to aim the whole tank.

148
00:13:15,240 --> 00:13:19,820
These mobile machine gun nests took the advantage away from the defenders

149
00:13:19,821 --> 00:13:23,160
and gave attackers a chance to gain and hold territory.

150
00:13:24,520 --> 00:13:28,600
As tanks moved ahead, the infantry could also move forward

151
00:13:28,601 --> 00:13:31,600
without leaving behind the firepower of their machine guns.

152
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:35,780
A new lightweight rapid-fire weapon gave soldiers an opportunity

153
00:13:35,781 --> 00:13:38,500
to exploit a breakthrough made by tanks.

154
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:41,720
Developed in the closing months of the war,

155
00:13:42,020 --> 00:13:44,780
John Browning's automatic rifle gave the infantrymen,

156
00:13:44,860 --> 00:13:46,280
used to single-shot rifles,

157
00:13:46,740 --> 00:13:49,760
a way to sustain a high rate of fire while on the move.

158
00:13:51,720 --> 00:13:55,120
The BAR would soon redefine infantry tactics.

159
00:13:55,121 --> 00:14:01,500
The Browning Automatic Marvel replaces the show show as a base of fire weapon.

160
00:14:02,280 --> 00:14:06,140
Infantry tactics revolve around gaining fire superiority.

161
00:14:06,820 --> 00:14:14,760
So what you want to do is to lay down a base of fire so you can maneuver an element against the enemy.

162
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:20,740
Each squad carried one BAR.

163
00:14:20,741 --> 00:14:25,720
The man handling the BAR was responsible for maintaining a sustained rate of fire

164
00:14:25,721 --> 00:14:29,200
so that his squad members could maneuver against their objective.

165
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:33,560
It was a good bit heavier than a rifle,

166
00:14:34,380 --> 00:14:37,860
but it was extremely effective as an infantry weapon because a man could carry it with him

167
00:14:37,861 --> 00:14:41,460
and move it forward and support the infantry when they were trying to advance

168
00:14:41,461 --> 00:14:43,840
or could also support them when they were trying to defend themselves.

169
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:47,700
It fired just like a machine gun except it didn't have as many rounds to fire.

170
00:14:47,701 --> 00:14:53,760
The weapon's barrel could not be changed, and the magazine held only 20 rounds,

171
00:14:54,200 --> 00:14:57,580
so there was no real capability for sustaining a high rate of fire.

172
00:15:00,220 --> 00:15:03,720
But the .30 caliber BAR was very rugged and reliable.

173
00:15:04,740 --> 00:15:07,080
In World War II, it was used on all fronts

174
00:15:07,081 --> 00:15:10,600
whenever the infantry could use the additional firepower that it could deliver.

175
00:15:17,700 --> 00:15:30,340
The British Army used similar infantry tactics,

176
00:15:30,640 --> 00:15:34,420
but they counted on the best magazine-fed light machine gun used during the war,

177
00:15:34,780 --> 00:15:37,040
the simple, reliable Bren gun.

178
00:15:40,140 --> 00:15:43,280
Interesting thing about the Bren gun is that it's not even British.

179
00:15:43,960 --> 00:15:46,720
It was a Czech light machine gun.

180
00:15:47,700 --> 00:15:51,180
The British thought enough of the weapon to produce it under license

181
00:15:51,181 --> 00:15:59,240
and called it the Bren from Brno, the Czech town where it was designed,

182
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:02,240
and Enfield, the British armory that built it.

183
00:16:03,040 --> 00:16:05,940
It was air-cooled, relatively light.

184
00:16:06,540 --> 00:16:09,280
It had a fairly low rate of fire, but was quite reliable.

185
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:13,880
It was fed by a 30-round box magazine mounted on top of the receiver,

186
00:16:13,881 --> 00:16:19,900
and it was as well-loved in the British Army as the BAR was in the American Army.

187
00:16:22,020 --> 00:16:26,300
When you're on your stomach, it's easier to load, of course, from the top than it is from the bottom.

188
00:16:26,740 --> 00:16:32,280
If you think about it, loading from the top is using gravity to your advantage.

189
00:16:33,960 --> 00:16:37,400
The Bren also offered the important advantage of a quick-change barrel.

190
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:41,140
A hot barrel could be quickly removed and replaced with a spare.

191
00:16:41,780 --> 00:16:45,400
With this feature, the Bren could continue firing and not overheat.

192
00:16:50,740 --> 00:16:55,740
When the British required more firepower, they moved up their heavy Vickers machine guns.

193
00:16:56,380 --> 00:16:59,980
When the Americans needed more firepower than the BAR could provide,

194
00:16:59,981 --> 00:17:03,920
they turned to the belt-fed .30 caliber Browning medium machine gun.

195
00:17:03,921 --> 00:17:16,500
A magazine-fed weapon, you fire, say, 30, 40, 50 rounds, whatever you have in the magazine.

196
00:17:16,900 --> 00:17:18,060
Then you have to change the magazine.

197
00:17:18,760 --> 00:17:22,460
With a belt-fed weapon, you can fire as many rounds as you wish.

198
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:30,980
The advantage is you get more sustained firepower than you do from a magazine-fed weapon.

199
00:17:30,981 --> 00:17:35,260
Introduced by John Browning in the last days of World War I,

200
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:37,200
the guns came in two versions,

201
00:17:37,520 --> 00:17:40,420
a water-cooled version that circulated water around the barrel

202
00:17:40,421 --> 00:17:42,720
and a second type cooled by air.

203
00:17:44,620 --> 00:17:47,260
The guns were essentially the same except the water-cooled gun.

204
00:17:47,340 --> 00:17:49,500
You could fire it a whole lot longer without the barrel getting too hot.

205
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:52,080
Whereas the air-cooled gun, you could fire it somewhere around there

206
00:17:52,081 --> 00:17:54,900
and let it cool off a little bit, and then you could fire it somewhere.

207
00:17:55,180 --> 00:17:56,500
But they were both excellent guns.

208
00:17:56,501 --> 00:18:08,420
Both guns, the M1917 and the M1919, could sustain a rate of fire as high as 600 rounds a minute to an effective range of about 500 yards.

209
00:18:09,020 --> 00:18:16,000
With simple recoil mechanisms, they were particularly rugged weapons under extreme conditions of temperature, dust, or mud.

210
00:18:18,100 --> 00:18:26,120
These guns were used primarily against heavy troop attacks and to command such strategic areas as bridges, streets, and hilltops.

211
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:31,860
68,000 of Browning's machine guns saw combat action.

212
00:18:35,900 --> 00:18:36,600
Ready?

213
00:18:37,540 --> 00:18:38,240
What?

214
00:18:45,240 --> 00:18:46,640
How come he didn't get back?

215
00:18:48,660 --> 00:18:49,780
Almost got deep.

216
00:18:50,140 --> 00:18:55,000
Today, half of the country's machine guns are owned by law enforcement and government agencies.

217
00:18:55,001 --> 00:18:57,640
Machine Guns will return on Modern Marvels.

218
00:19:02,760 --> 00:19:08,380
The American army used its machine guns as a supplement to the aimed fire of the individual rifleman.

219
00:19:09,080 --> 00:19:12,520
The machine gunner was there to help the infantryman reach his objective.

220
00:19:13,220 --> 00:19:16,200
But in the German army, that relationship was reversed.

221
00:19:16,840 --> 00:19:21,120
The German infantryman was there to ensure that the machine gunner achieved his goal.

222
00:19:22,040 --> 00:19:27,800
Uninhibited by conventional thought, German leaders conceived of a new way to employ their machine guns.

223
00:19:28,420 --> 00:19:33,160
In the German army, the doctrine that developed was that the like machine gun

224
00:19:33,161 --> 00:19:39,580
would be the standard field-of-fire weapon and would be supported by infantry

225
00:19:39,581 --> 00:19:43,040
who would take out targets that the machine guns did not hit,

226
00:19:44,040 --> 00:19:47,060
be responsible for supporting the machine gun, protecting it,

227
00:19:47,061 --> 00:19:52,700
and as a result the German army had a far greater complement of machine guns

228
00:19:52,701 --> 00:19:55,080
than most allied armies of the period did.

229
00:19:56,760 --> 00:20:03,660
One machine gun is as good as maybe 20 single-shot riflemen

230
00:20:03,661 --> 00:20:07,380
and the firepower is enormous.

231
00:20:07,780 --> 00:20:14,360
One machine gun could hold down a whole section of the enemy line

232
00:20:14,361 --> 00:20:20,680
and the rest of the company was able to advance under this firepower forward.

233
00:20:22,560 --> 00:20:29,560
The weapon designed to implement this new doctrine was the Machin Gewehr 34, the MG 34.

234
00:20:31,660 --> 00:20:35,820
The German MG 34 was the masterpiece of design.

235
00:20:36,880 --> 00:20:40,480
The Germans wanted to develop a weapon that was versatile enough

236
00:20:40,481 --> 00:20:43,480
that it could serve the function of a heavy machine gun.

237
00:20:44,360 --> 00:20:48,880
and still have the portability of a light machine gun.

238
00:20:49,400 --> 00:20:53,980
The aspect of the design that enabled this convertibility, if you will,

239
00:20:54,580 --> 00:20:56,340
was a quick-change barrel.

240
00:20:57,340 --> 00:20:59,340
In rapid firing, the barrel gets hot,

241
00:20:59,560 --> 00:21:02,180
so you have to change it after every 300 rounds or so.

242
00:21:02,740 --> 00:21:05,580
And with that one, you just flipped one bottom on the side,

243
00:21:05,620 --> 00:21:06,540
and the barrel came out.

244
00:21:06,541 --> 00:21:08,800
It was red hot, so you had to have some gloves,

245
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:12,560
put it on the side, let it cool off, and put in the next one.

246
00:21:12,561 --> 00:21:17,420
and this added to the firepower in comparison to American machine guns.

247
00:21:17,500 --> 00:21:20,240
I guess you needed a screwdriver to take it apart, change the barrel,

248
00:21:20,241 --> 00:21:25,560
and it took at least five times as long to change the barrel in combat.

249
00:21:27,420 --> 00:21:30,620
Most machine guns were issued with two or three spare barrels

250
00:21:30,621 --> 00:21:34,060
which enabled the crew to rotate them through as needed.

251
00:21:34,580 --> 00:21:40,320
And it was not unusual for German MG34s to fire thousands of rounds of ammunition

252
00:21:40,321 --> 00:21:43,280
in a day of heavy engagements.

253
00:21:44,680 --> 00:21:49,100
The Germans also replaced the old canvas ammo belts with a new device.

254
00:21:50,320 --> 00:21:54,680
It was a metal belt, which could be reloaded,

255
00:21:55,260 --> 00:21:57,120
and therefore it didn't bend so much.

256
00:21:57,560 --> 00:22:03,340
It was quite heavy, but it was, for this reason, also more dependable,

257
00:22:03,540 --> 00:22:08,160
and we didn't mind the weight because we knew it would shoot.

258
00:22:08,161 --> 00:22:16,100
This machine gun was our main source of firepower for the combat unit.

259
00:22:17,760 --> 00:22:19,660
You stick it in the back seat of an aircraft.

260
00:22:20,400 --> 00:22:22,080
You can stick it on top of a tank.

261
00:22:22,760 --> 00:22:25,140
You can stick it in the hands of the infantry.

262
00:22:25,860 --> 00:22:32,540
So it becomes sort of a universal gun that can do many, many different jobs and do them effectively.

263
00:22:32,541 --> 00:22:40,380
But too few factories could machine the complicated parts required for the MG 34.

264
00:22:41,700 --> 00:22:44,940
Production could not meet the growing demand of the German army.

265
00:22:44,941 --> 00:22:58,941
The MG42 was designed at the request of the German army who wanted a weapon that would be cheaper to build and more reliable under severe combat conditions than the

266
00:23:01,322 --> 00:23:03,620
very well-crafted MG34.

267
00:23:04,580 --> 00:23:07,140
Relatively few complicated parts were used.

268
00:23:08,040 --> 00:23:10,740
And in an attempt to make the weapon even more versatile,

269
00:23:11,560 --> 00:23:21,020
the barrel design was altered so that now the barrel could be ejected from the side of the barrel jacket.

270
00:23:21,400 --> 00:23:27,200
It took less than 10 seconds to change the barrel on an MG42, and the gun was ready to fire.

271
00:23:27,920 --> 00:23:31,060
It was marginally lighter than the MG34.

272
00:23:31,061 --> 00:23:36,420
it was not as well made, its tolerances were looser, it was not quite as accurate

273
00:23:36,421 --> 00:23:38,800
but it was far more reliable

274
00:23:38,801 --> 00:23:45,820
and the rate of fire was increased to between 1200 and 1500 rounds a minute.

275
00:23:47,040 --> 00:23:50,440
The rate of fire was higher than any other gun ever produced

276
00:23:50,441 --> 00:23:52,620
but it cost the gun its accuracy.

277
00:23:53,360 --> 00:23:57,680
Once the first rounds left the barrel, the excessive recoil shook the gun out of control.

278
00:23:57,681 --> 00:24:01,500
you had to lie down

279
00:24:01,501 --> 00:24:06,080
it wasn't possible to walk with it or to kneel

280
00:24:06,081 --> 00:24:08,300
because the recoil was so strong

281
00:24:08,301 --> 00:24:11,220
that an average man of my weight

282
00:24:11,221 --> 00:24:13,220
would have been pushed backwards

283
00:24:13,221 --> 00:24:17,920
the idea really was to use the weapon

284
00:24:17,921 --> 00:24:20,420
under ideal circumstances

285
00:24:20,421 --> 00:24:22,300
as a very long range shotgun

286
00:24:22,301 --> 00:24:25,160
firing a burst of rounds

287
00:24:25,161 --> 00:24:29,780
that would hit in the impact area almost at the same time.

288
00:24:30,880 --> 00:24:33,320
Most of the times we didn't even have to aim.

289
00:24:33,440 --> 00:24:37,420
We just were shooting with it over the heads of the enemy

290
00:24:37,421 --> 00:24:41,720
and they just kept running away because they could not stand that roar.

291
00:24:42,400 --> 00:24:47,240
They were shooting so fast that our ear could not register single explosions

292
00:24:47,241 --> 00:24:50,180
like a normal machine gun, like the 34.

293
00:24:51,460 --> 00:24:53,400
But it was just a roar.

294
00:24:53,820 --> 00:24:54,520
Wow!

295
00:24:55,160 --> 00:25:00,300
Wow! Half a second and you had out about 100 bullets.

296
00:25:02,880 --> 00:25:06,420
The roar and the high rate of fire were thought to be so intimidating

297
00:25:06,421 --> 00:25:10,820
that the U.S. Army produced a film that they had hoped would prove to new recruits.

298
00:25:11,220 --> 00:25:12,360
They had nothing to fear.

299
00:25:16,020 --> 00:25:21,180
Listen to that. Fast. That thing sprays a lot of lead.

300
00:25:21,181 --> 00:25:29,100
and you're scared because the German gun fires faster than anything you've run into before

301
00:25:29,101 --> 00:25:35,960
nobody seems especially afraid of that gun nobody but the replacement who can't get over the fast

302
00:25:35,961 --> 00:25:43,240
burp of that German gun well so it does have a high rate of fire does that mean it's a better

303
00:25:43,241 --> 00:25:44,360
fighting weapon than ours?

304
00:25:45,560 --> 00:25:48,560
The main disadvantage of the machine gun 42

305
00:25:48,561 --> 00:25:52,980
was the fact that it used so much ammunition.

306
00:25:53,940 --> 00:25:57,780
And so we were only permitted to shoot very short times

307
00:25:57,781 --> 00:26:00,240
to pull the trigger not longer than a second

308
00:26:00,241 --> 00:26:03,840
because 50 to 100 rounds were going out like nothing.

309
00:26:04,380 --> 00:26:06,700
And you run out of ammunition.

310
00:26:08,160 --> 00:26:11,520
The German concept of developing one machine gun

311
00:26:11,521 --> 00:26:13,340
that could meet a variety of needs

312
00:26:13,341 --> 00:26:16,040
influenced all future machine gun development.

313
00:26:32,620 --> 00:26:34,540
The German gun shoots fast.

314
00:26:39,740 --> 00:26:40,440
Correction.

315
00:26:41,520 --> 00:26:43,920
The German gun used to shoot that.

316
00:26:44,960 --> 00:26:47,960
That's the way an enemy machine gun position should look.

317
00:26:49,100 --> 00:26:53,280
Remember, their bar is worse than their bite.

318
00:26:54,780 --> 00:26:59,660
Since 1934, only one legally owned machine gun has been used in a murder,

319
00:26:59,980 --> 00:27:02,240
and a law enforcement officer committed that crime.

320
00:27:02,700 --> 00:27:04,180
Modern Marvel will return in a moment.

321
00:27:05,800 --> 00:27:09,600
The heaviest machine gun employed by the U.S. Army in World War II

322
00:27:09,601 --> 00:27:13,980
was an enlarged version of Browning's .30 caliber medium machine gun.

323
00:27:14,920 --> 00:27:20,340
The .50 caliber M2 heavy machine gun fired a very powerful half-inch diameter lead slug

324
00:27:20,341 --> 00:27:22,960
nearly 2,000 yards accurately.

325
00:27:25,340 --> 00:27:29,880
Interestingly enough, when it appeared, it was first classified as an anti-tank gun.

326
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:34,560
In the 1920s, the .50 caliber Browning would have been effective

327
00:27:34,561 --> 00:27:37,820
against the overwhelming majority of tanks

328
00:27:37,821 --> 00:27:40,840
and certainly all of the armored cars that were in service.

329
00:27:42,720 --> 00:27:45,100
A .50-cowl was really a powerful weapon.

330
00:27:45,840 --> 00:27:50,620
Most of our machine gun ammunition was loaded in repetitive steps of five rounds each.

331
00:27:51,420 --> 00:27:52,920
You had two rounds of ball ammunition.

332
00:27:53,060 --> 00:27:57,260
Ball ammunition is just plain lead with a steel jacket and a copper covering over it,

333
00:27:57,300 --> 00:27:58,360
and that's used against personnel.

334
00:27:59,360 --> 00:28:03,280
Then you'd have one round of incendiary ammunition, white burning phosphorus,

335
00:28:03,560 --> 00:28:06,080
which when you shoot in a gas tank or something like that, it's set on fire.

336
00:28:06,081 --> 00:28:08,020
then you had a tracer

337
00:28:08,021 --> 00:28:10,120
which would put out a red marker

338
00:28:10,121 --> 00:28:12,820
what it does

339
00:28:12,821 --> 00:28:15,100
it leaves a trail of white hot light

340
00:28:15,101 --> 00:28:16,620
which the gunner can then use

341
00:28:16,621 --> 00:28:18,780
to determine his exact fall of fire

342
00:28:18,781 --> 00:28:20,340
the disadvantage

343
00:28:20,341 --> 00:28:22,300
of tracers

344
00:28:22,301 --> 00:28:24,520
is that while they are very useful

345
00:28:24,521 --> 00:28:26,840
for showing you where your fire is going

346
00:28:26,841 --> 00:28:28,540
they also show the enemy

347
00:28:28,541 --> 00:28:29,680
where your fire is going

348
00:28:29,681 --> 00:28:33,580
and then we have one round of armor piercing

349
00:28:33,581 --> 00:28:35,140
the 50 cap would go through

350
00:28:35,141 --> 00:28:38,320
a half to three quarters of an inch and would do a pretty good job of one inch of armor.

351
00:28:38,460 --> 00:28:39,680
It wouldn't quite go all the way through it.

352
00:28:40,400 --> 00:28:44,800
And you could knock out a German armored car or a lightly armored vehicle, but it wouldn't

353
00:28:44,801 --> 00:28:45,520
go through a tank.

354
00:28:47,440 --> 00:28:52,760
The Browning had a normal rate of fire of about 500 rounds per minute.

355
00:28:53,340 --> 00:28:57,360
The rate of fire was sufficiently slow that a good gunner could tap off one or two rounds

356
00:28:57,361 --> 00:29:00,460
fairly reliably just by hitting the trigger with a fairly light touch.

357
00:29:01,860 --> 00:29:03,600
The Germans were terrified of that weapon.

358
00:29:03,601 --> 00:29:05,340
They absolutely, you could hear it.

359
00:29:05,480 --> 00:29:08,200
It had a very different sound than a .30 caliber.

360
00:29:08,880 --> 00:29:12,220
We had an incident one time where we had just gotten in Normandy.

361
00:29:12,320 --> 00:29:13,400
It was the first day we were in combat.

362
00:29:14,460 --> 00:29:18,240
And we were trying to maintain our tanks and trying to fix them.

363
00:29:18,480 --> 00:29:21,100
And all of a sudden, we got this sniper fire coming in.

364
00:29:21,280 --> 00:29:23,420
And everybody hits the damn deck and hits the ground.

365
00:29:23,520 --> 00:29:26,180
And the guys had left their damn rifles over the truck somewhere.

366
00:29:26,280 --> 00:29:27,380
And they couldn't find their rifles.

367
00:29:28,280 --> 00:29:29,640
Everybody was in kind of panic.

368
00:29:30,560 --> 00:29:34,160
And so then they got up again to get another shot.

369
00:29:34,960 --> 00:29:38,220
A sniper find is very discouraging because you can't tell where it's coming from.

370
00:29:38,300 --> 00:29:39,800
You don't have any idea what direction it is.

371
00:29:40,320 --> 00:29:44,240
So a single sniper just completely stopped the whole platoon from working.

372
00:29:44,980 --> 00:29:47,900
One of our guys thought it should come across the street in a big pine tree.

373
00:29:48,660 --> 00:29:53,840
So about that time, a half-track came down the road and had a 50-cal machine gun.

374
00:29:54,800 --> 00:29:57,520
And so they saw our crew paying down and they slowed down.

375
00:29:57,521 --> 00:29:59,440
and one of our guys pointed over there to the tree

376
00:29:59,441 --> 00:30:02,440
and the man on the ring, the 50 caliber, must have spotted him

377
00:30:02,441 --> 00:30:04,400
because he swung that 50 caliber out of the 50 caliber

378
00:30:04,401 --> 00:30:07,420
and the whole top of that tree just exploded

379
00:30:07,421 --> 00:30:10,500
and the sniper came down to the ground and that was the end of that.

380
00:30:11,520 --> 00:30:14,680
And so I began to realize that that 50 caliber put a damn awesome weapon.

381
00:30:17,280 --> 00:30:20,100
The long range and heavy bullet of the 50 caliber

382
00:30:20,101 --> 00:30:24,540
made it an obvious choice when the Army was designing an anti-aircraft weapon.

383
00:30:25,220 --> 00:30:27,840
Four guns were mounted on a highly mobile half-track.

384
00:30:30,980 --> 00:30:36,100
We had one anti-aircraft battalion attached to each Army division.

385
00:30:36,760 --> 00:30:41,440
They had the M-15 half-track, which had four .50-cal machine guns on it.

386
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:43,600
They could be used for anti-aircraft fire.

387
00:30:43,780 --> 00:30:46,480
They could also be used for anti-personnel fire.

388
00:30:47,260 --> 00:30:49,300
And these systems were deadly.

389
00:30:49,301 --> 00:30:59,040
The electrically controlled gun turret could rotate 360 degrees and elevate 90 degrees

390
00:30:59,041 --> 00:31:04,980
The guns were modified so that ammunition could be fed from either side

391
00:31:04,981 --> 00:31:11,000
When fired together, the four machine guns could send over 2,000 rounds a minute

392
00:31:11,001 --> 00:31:13,040
To meet any attacking aircraft

393
00:31:13,041 --> 00:31:18,460
The Quad 50 was also turned against ground targets

394
00:31:18,461 --> 00:31:28,920
war industries also produced thousands of 50 caliber machine guns to give combat aircraft a greater punch

395
00:31:28,921 --> 00:31:38,280
this combination of firepower and fighter was unmatched in the p-51 mustang

396
00:31:38,281 --> 00:31:52,281
the p-51 was a fine airplane it had six forward firing machine guns we had a lot of latitude as

397
00:31:52,720 --> 00:31:59,620
to how we want those guns to fire and some guys wanted those 650s to converge on a point out

398
00:31:59,621 --> 00:32:05,660
there the size of a four-bit piece at 300 yards all six guns passing through that same very

399
00:32:05,661 --> 00:32:08,800
small diameter, which gives you a tremendous impact.

400
00:32:09,400 --> 00:32:13,820
Other guys wanted the outside guns to reach out to 350 yards,

401
00:32:14,240 --> 00:32:17,900
and his middle guns at 3 1⁄4,

402
00:32:18,040 --> 00:32:21,400
and his inside guns maybe as close as 250 yards,

403
00:32:21,401 --> 00:32:23,500
which gave him a long cone of fire.

404
00:32:25,700 --> 00:32:29,620
I like to have them all passing through a little dot out there

405
00:32:29,621 --> 00:32:30,520
that I could barely see.

406
00:32:30,521 --> 00:32:38,140
fighter aircraft used their guns offensively to take the fight to the enemy

407
00:32:38,141 --> 00:32:46,040
but bombers like the b-17 used their guns for defense

408
00:32:46,041 --> 00:32:55,760
1350 caliber guns justifiably earned the b-17 its nickname a flying fortress

409
00:32:55,761 --> 00:33:02,400
the guns bristled from all around the aircraft

410
00:33:02,401 --> 00:33:04,940
from nose positions, waist positions

411
00:33:04,941 --> 00:33:07,780
to twin turrets that could follow the attacking fighters

412
00:33:07,781 --> 00:33:16,280
no matter how skilled the aerial gunners

413
00:33:16,281 --> 00:33:18,820
it was never easy to hit the fast moving

414
00:33:18,821 --> 00:33:20,380
quick turning German planes

415
00:33:20,381 --> 00:33:24,220
a better system for aiming the defensive guns

416
00:33:24,221 --> 00:33:29,000
was incorporated in a new bomber, a long-range B-29 Superfortress.

417
00:33:32,280 --> 00:33:37,080
There was no handheld guns which were ineffective against fighter attacks,

418
00:33:37,100 --> 00:33:38,240
no matter how you say it.

419
00:33:38,560 --> 00:33:41,500
The effectiveness of our guns was much different.

420
00:33:41,760 --> 00:33:44,180
We had remote-controlled guns.

421
00:33:44,760 --> 00:33:48,160
We didn't sit in a turret, you know, on a B-29 like they did in a B-17.

422
00:33:48,480 --> 00:33:53,040
The navigator had to merely set the altitude, speed, and temperature into the computer.

423
00:33:53,041 --> 00:33:59,680
and the computer would take care of all the ballistics as far as firing at it in the airplane.

424
00:34:00,220 --> 00:34:04,800
You had a sight that had a little 70-mil ring sight on it.

425
00:34:05,180 --> 00:34:10,280
The gunner got that sucker on the enemy aircraft and he was within range.

426
00:34:10,360 --> 00:34:12,140
He's probably going to hit that airplane.

427
00:34:17,480 --> 00:34:22,740
From its role as an anti-aircraft weapon, anti-tank weapon, and heavy defense weapon,

428
00:34:23,040 --> 00:34:29,000
The .50 caliber Browning proved itself as one of the most versatile and effective machine guns fielded in combat.

429
00:34:33,340 --> 00:34:42,500
In August 1999, the Los Angeles County Board of Supervisors voted for the first time in 30 years to ban the Great Western Gun Show.

430
00:34:43,020 --> 00:34:45,360
Machine Guns will return on Modern Marbles.

431
00:34:45,361 --> 00:34:54,620
The M250 caliber and the smaller M191730 caliber

432
00:34:54,621 --> 00:34:56,027
served with the U.S.

433
00:34:56,028 --> 00:35:00,600
Army basically unchanged in the Korean War and through the 1950s.

434
00:35:00,601 --> 00:35:04,240
The unparalleled reliability of the Browning machine guns

435
00:35:04,241 --> 00:35:07,320
was the reason for their long successful service record.

436
00:35:07,321 --> 00:35:17,120
Following the Korean War, the rifleman's M1 Garand rifle was replaced with the M14 rifle.

437
00:35:17,720 --> 00:35:22,840
Unlike the M1, the M14 could fire in bursts or fully automatic like the BAR.

438
00:35:23,800 --> 00:35:29,780
It was also time to replace the older machine guns with more capable, lighter, base-of-fire weapons.

439
00:35:29,781 --> 00:35:38,960
During the Second World War, Americans captured examples of the MG42 in North Africa.

440
00:35:40,000 --> 00:35:42,460
They were extremely impressed with the weapon.

441
00:35:42,800 --> 00:35:48,420
Although they felt the rate of fire was too high, they marveled at its simplicity, its reliability, and its ease of manufacture.

442
00:35:50,240 --> 00:35:57,760
These important features, which proved very successful for the Germans' MG42, were carefully tested and re-examined.

443
00:35:57,761 --> 00:36:03,080
When it comes time for us to redesign a new machine gun with interchangeable barrel,

444
00:36:03,880 --> 00:36:12,180
our designers take the German idea of the feed plate and feed mechanism in the MG42

445
00:36:12,181 --> 00:36:17,280
and design our gun around that particular feed mechanism.

446
00:36:19,060 --> 00:36:24,140
The M60 general purpose machine gun first introduced in 1959

447
00:36:24,141 --> 00:36:30,320
used the reliable gas-driven bolt action of the World War I Lewis gun and BAR,

448
00:36:31,220 --> 00:36:33,640
the feed mechanism of the German MG42,

449
00:36:34,160 --> 00:36:37,220
and the quick-change barrel feature of the British Bren gun.

450
00:36:39,220 --> 00:36:44,220
If you open up the feed plate cover of an M60 and the MG42,

451
00:36:45,140 --> 00:36:46,920
you're going to find that they're going to be identical.

452
00:36:48,520 --> 00:36:51,560
At 550 to 600 rounds a minute,

453
00:36:51,561 --> 00:36:56,740
the rate of fire of the M60 was not that much higher than the weapons it replaced.

454
00:36:57,940 --> 00:37:02,600
But the improved design was much better suited to the requirements of modern combat.

455
00:37:06,400 --> 00:37:09,820
But early tests of the first M60s revealed flaws.

456
00:37:11,460 --> 00:37:12,920
There are a few problems with it.

457
00:37:13,160 --> 00:37:17,640
One of the things is that you cannot change the barrel with a handle.

458
00:37:17,920 --> 00:37:21,280
You have to grab the thing with a mitt that's issued with the gun.

459
00:37:21,560 --> 00:37:23,460
But it's still a very good gun.

460
00:37:23,860 --> 00:37:25,600
Used to great effect in Vietnam.

461
00:37:30,540 --> 00:37:34,480
In jungles of Vietnam, firefights came fast and furious.

462
00:37:37,020 --> 00:37:40,140
Firepower had to be brought to bear quickly and accurately.

463
00:37:42,620 --> 00:37:46,480
The M60 machine gun was the weapon that often made the difference

464
00:37:46,481 --> 00:37:48,580
for the soldiers and Marines on the battlefield.

465
00:37:51,560 --> 00:38:05,560
Variants of the M60 could be fitted above the skids of Huey helicopters.

466
00:38:10,820 --> 00:38:15,420
A siding linkage allowed the guns to be aimed and fired from inside the cockpit.

467
00:38:16,220 --> 00:38:21,240
M60 machine guns turned the vulnerable troop transports into lethal gunships.

468
00:38:21,560 --> 00:38:35,560
Machine guns mounted in the open doors gave gunners greater flexibility.

469
00:38:42,720 --> 00:38:46,480
M60s pouring thousands of rounds into the dense green jungle

470
00:38:46,481 --> 00:38:50,640
seemed almost symbolic of the frustrations of fighting the war in Vietnam.

471
00:38:51,560 --> 00:39:00,140
One of the most interesting developments in the past few decades has been the rebirth of the Gatling gun.

472
00:39:00,940 --> 00:39:13,780
In the 1950s and 60s, the Air Force in particular was interested in developing weapons that had a higher rate of fire than the traditional aircraft machine gun.

473
00:39:13,781 --> 00:39:25,893
The Gatling proved to be the perfect answer in that with a six-barrel Gatling gun firing at a rate of, say, 3,600 rounds a minute, each barrel was being fired only at

474
00:39:25,894 --> 00:39:28,520
a 600-round-per-minute rate of fire.

475
00:39:29,260 --> 00:39:36,260
With the blast of air going down each open board, this was sufficiently slow to keep the weapon cool enough to operate.

476
00:39:36,261 --> 00:39:44,260
multi-barreled rotary machine guns were used in many ways and offered one other tremendous advantage

477
00:39:44,261 --> 00:39:52,840
powered by an external source it was not affected by duds or misfires

478
00:39:52,841 --> 00:39:59,320
the weapon simply ejected the live cartridge case that had not fired along with the empties

479
00:40:06,260 --> 00:40:16,600
On the A-10 Warthog, a 30-millimeter Gatling gun puts the bust in Tank Buster,

480
00:40:17,260 --> 00:40:22,220
firing armor-piercing shells of depleted uranium at up to 4,000 rounds a minute.

481
00:40:22,820 --> 00:40:26,860
The A-10's gun decimated Iraqi armor during the Persian Gulf War.

482
00:40:29,440 --> 00:40:32,580
Today's infantry depends on many familiar weapons.

483
00:40:32,581 --> 00:40:40,980
The M250 caliber still lives up to its reputation, and the M60 general-purpose machine gun remains a base of fire weapon.

484
00:40:43,100 --> 00:40:47,920
Withdrawn from the inventory after Vietnam but recently returned to use by the Marines,

485
00:40:48,480 --> 00:40:54,180
the Mark 19 launches 40-millimeter grenades at a rate of 375 rounds a minute.

486
00:40:56,320 --> 00:41:01,620
A new weapon that significantly boosts the firepower of the Marines is the SAW,

487
00:41:01,621 --> 00:41:03,460
The squad automatic weapon.

488
00:41:04,220 --> 00:41:08,040
The squad automatic weapon, it's called the M249 squad automatic weapon.

489
00:41:08,200 --> 00:41:12,580
It shoots a 5.56 bullet, which is the same bullet that's also fired by an M16.

490
00:41:13,160 --> 00:41:15,300
The ammunition comes in a drone of 200 rounds.

491
00:41:15,880 --> 00:41:18,540
It also can fire the same magazine that an M16 fires.

492
00:41:20,420 --> 00:41:22,640
It's an air-cooled, strictly automatic weapon,

493
00:41:22,860 --> 00:41:28,180
and that weapon is used primarily to suppress enemy targets in order to hold their heads down

494
00:41:28,181 --> 00:41:30,620
while the Marines are moving up onto an objective.

495
00:41:31,620 --> 00:41:37,680
A Marine squad consists of 13 men, a leader, and three four-man fire teams.

496
00:41:37,900 --> 00:41:43,360
They alternate rushing and objectives so that not all of the men are exposed to enemy fire at the same time.

497
00:41:43,900 --> 00:41:46,820
Each man uses his weapon to cover the advance of the others.

498
00:41:53,280 --> 00:41:56,680
Three members of each fire team carry the M16 rifle.

499
00:41:56,681 --> 00:42:04,680
And then the backbone or the firepower, the main punch of that fire team is the last man who carries the squad automatic weapon.

500
00:42:06,020 --> 00:42:12,280
The lethality of the modern battlefield could send infantrymen fleeing for trenches like their World War I forefathers.

501
00:42:12,840 --> 00:42:19,220
But it is the machine gun, mobile firepower, that keeps the infantry moving forward to win their objective.

502
00:42:19,221 --> 00:42:26,800
Tonight on History's Mystery Saturday,

503
00:42:27,220 --> 00:42:31,140
this guy from New Jersey is getting a crash course in surviving a vacation from hell.

504
00:42:31,500 --> 00:42:35,680
You kind of wish he paid more attention to the State Department warning about guerrilla kidnappings in Ecuador.

505
00:42:36,220 --> 00:42:39,300
But if you can just recall one or two things now about counterterror,

506
00:42:39,560 --> 00:42:41,920
he could psych out his captors and come home alive.

507
00:42:42,420 --> 00:42:43,900
Tonight at 9 on the History Channel.

